WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:05.725
A new concept: delegated authority. You already know what it means to be able to edit a calendar. Delegated authority is the specific permission to make a decision or act within an agreed scope and conditions. Being able to use the system is a different fact. The two situations below help you decide whether Daniel is permitted to act. In the first, all conditions of the supplied delegation are met. In the second, system access exists but decision authority does not. Compare the permission and conditions, not how easy the action would be to carry out. Within the supplied delegation. Daniel moves a routine internal administrative catch-up inside an agreed free period. No protected commitment , external undertaking, cost or specialist dependency is affected, and the actual delegation permits it. Not established by access. Daniel can edit a supplier record. That does not authorise him to approve a bank-detail change. The appointed finance owner must handle the supplied verification/decision route. The next classification activity lets you apply the distinction. The changed case in Activity 16 checks whether you can use it outside the diary example.
